#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074958 :tutorial/content "Fraction multiplication occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a bag contains ![](images/bc/bcc2ab102a29294d71449d99af1cf1a88aae88047223f829175086f7a6cd5e10.svg) of flour. If Nancy used ![](images/c0/c0db422914dd04be9adf05af649ec06419e80d8425cd0a85f6105cef7cc447b5.svg) of the bag to make a pie, how much flour did she use to make the pie?\n\nTo find out how much flour Nancy used, we need to multiply ![](images/16/1627bb3010ada55ed941966d38081f1a6c1394c07ca78b94585f8c97e4bbc138.svg) by ![](images/d9/d989525f1462386565e344f7e9a41d48cea1e85148f9f5aba25268f589a8bcbc.svg)\n\nTo multiply these two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n![](images/58/58824e95f00448b5c7d509f0781a367506ccf37fa018f2a0ba373c74191f1bb6.svg)\n\nTherefore, Nancy used ![](images/fd/fd1a8efcb6ae625f2259a43e8ffeb93d486d409af6175b6e52380f4ccd488af7.svg) kilograms of flour." :tutorial/id #uuid "a638bc3c-9858-5cb6-a95b-ba0a356c4d1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1894 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
